## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to simplify tagging, maintain per-env isolation, and introduce a safe migration path.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform provider default_tags
- Per-env backend prefixes
- Gate checks on plan output for tag presence
- Migration risk and rollback considerations

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  default_tags = {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
    }
  }
}
```

```hcl
# migrated resource sample (no wrapper)
resource "aws_vpc" "main" { cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16" }
```

```hcl
# backend example
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "corp-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle prod tag overrides if needed?
- What tooling would you add to enforce tag presence across environments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced Terraform design across multi-cloud per-tenant isolation, secret management, and policy enforcement. It also probes testing discipline and scalable module composition.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases per tenant
- Tenant-scoped remote backends with encryption
- Vault-backed credentials retrieval
- Per-tenant RBAC and network segmentation
- Idempotence testing with Terratest
- Policy gates with OPA or Sentinel

## Code Example

```hcl
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({ cloud = string, region = string }))
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "tenant_${each.key}"
  region = var.tenants[each.key].region
  # fetch creds from Vault here
}

provider "azurerm" {
  alias   = "tenant_${each.key}"
  features = {}
}

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key = "state/${each.key}/terraform.tfstate"
    encryption = true
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the design to support adding a new cloud to the tenant mix?
- How would you structure tests to run in parallel for many tenants?
